## Decoding the question
Four tasks. "**What is**" — a precise **definition**, ideally the ECOSOC wording. "**Discuss its tools**" — name and explain the **analytical** and **institutional** tools. "**Evaluate its achievements and limitations**" — both sides, then a judgement. "**As the strategy of GAD**" — link mainstreaming to GAD's aim of transforming **gender relations**. "**With reference to Pakistan**" — Pakistani examples throughout, not only at the end.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Gender mainstreaming** is the strategy of assessing the implications for women and men of **every** law, policy and programme, at every level, so that inequality is not reproduced. It is the main strategy of the **Gender and Development (GAD)** approach and was adopted worldwide by the **Beijing Platform for Action** (==1995==).

#### ii) The argument
Mainstreaming moved gender from the margins of development — small women's projects — to the **centre of policy**. It has put gender on every agenda, produced data, budgets and units, and in Pakistan helped create quotas, gender-responsive budgeting and cash transfers to women. But it has often become **technocratic** — checklists without power — and has been "**mainstreamed away**". It works only when tied to **political will, resources and women's own organisations**.

### 2. Why mainstreaming was needed
The **Women in Development (WID)** approach of the 1970s, inspired by **Ester Boserup** (==1970==), added women to development through separate projects — sewing centres, credit, training. These projects were small and **marginal**, while the big decisions on roads, budgets, trade and agriculture remained **gender-blind**. The **GAD** approach of the 1980s, shaped by socialist feminists and the Southern network **DAWN** (==1984==), argued that the problem was **unequal power** between women and men, embedded in every institution. If inequality is everywhere, the remedy must be everywhere too.

### 3. The definition and its origins
The UN **Economic and Social Council** defined mainstreaming in ==1997== as "the process of assessing the implications for women and men of any planned action, including legislation, policies or programmes, in all areas and at all levels". The **Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action** (==1995==) was the first global framework to adopt it, combining mainstreaming with **special measures** for women — the "**twin track**". It is meant not as an end but as a means to **gender equality** — today **SDG 5**.

::: fig flow | How gender mainstreaming is meant to work in a policy cycle.
@per 5
Data: count women and men separately
Analysis: who does what, who controls what
Design: policies that meet both needs
Budget: allocate and track by gender
Audit: measure results and correct
:::

### 4. Analytical tools

#### i) Sex-disaggregated data
Statistics separated by sex reveal what averages hide — for example, Pakistan's female labour-force participation of **24.4%** (LFS ==2024–25==) beside a far higher male rate.

#### ii) Gender analysis frameworks
The **Harvard Framework** (==1985==) maps **activities, access and control** of resources; **Caroline Moser**'s framework (==1989==, ==1993==) identifies women's **triple role** — productive, reproductive and community-managing — and separates **practical** from **strategic** gender needs; **Sara Longwe**'s framework ranks interventions from **welfare** to **control**; and **Naila Kabeer** (==1999==) defines empowerment through **resources, agency and achievements**.

### 5. Institutional tools

#### i) Gender-responsive budgeting
Analysing budgets by their effect on women and men, so that spending on maternal health, girls' schools or safe transport is visible and protected.

#### ii) Gender audits and impact assessment
Checking organisations and projects for bias before and after implementation.

#### iii) Focal points, units and quotas
**Gender focal points** in every ministry, women's machinery such as national commissions, and **quotas** that bring women into decision-making.

### 6. Achievements worldwide
Mainstreaming made gender the business of **every ministry**, not only a women's ministry. It produced a revolution in **sex-disaggregated data**, so that indices like the **Gender Development Index** (==1995==) and **Gender Inequality Index** (==2010==) became possible. Gender-responsive budgeting spread to many countries. Mainstreaming also shaped the **Millennium Development Goals** and **SDG 5**, and made donors require gender analysis in projects. In short, it changed the default question of policy from "what about women?" to "**how does this affect women and men?**"

### 7. Achievements in Pakistan
Pakistan adopted many of the tools. The **Gender Reform Action Plan** (early ==2000s==) sought to raise women's share of public jobs and create gender units in departments. The Ministry of Finance ran a **Gender Responsive Budgeting Initiative**. A **10% quota** for women in federal jobs, **60 reserved seats** in the National Assembly (==2002==) and **33%** of local seats (==2000–01==) brought women into the state. The **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==) reviews laws for their gender impact. The **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==) pays cash to **women**, giving them a resource in their own name. And strategic laws — the **Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act 2020** and the laws against violence — reflect GAD's aim of changing power, not only meeting needs.

### 8. Limitation 1: technocratic checklists
In practice mainstreaming is often reduced to **training workshops, checklists and reports**. Officials tick a "gender box" without changing what they do. Feminist critics warn that when **everyone** is responsible for gender, **no one** is — gender is "**mainstreamed away**". Budgets labelled "gender-responsive" may simply relabel existing spending.

### 9. Limitation 2: depoliticisation and donor dependence
GAD aimed to **transform power relations** — a political project. Mainstreaming, run by experts and consultants, can turn it into a **technical** exercise, stripped of the women's movement's demands. In Pakistan much gender work depends on **donor funding cycles**, so units and pilots fade when projects end — the "**NGO-isation**" critique. Gender-responsive budgeting pilots, for example, did not become a permanent feature of every budget.

### 10. Limitation 3: resistance, capacity and "gender" hiding women
Changing power provokes **resistance** from men and elites; governments avoid controversial strategic issues such as inheritance or marriage age. Ministries lack trained staff and reliable data, and after the **18th Amendment** (==2010==) provincial capacity varies widely. Talking of "gender" can also let agencies **cut women-specific programmes**. The outcomes show the gap between process and result: Pakistan ranked ==148th of 148== in the Global Gender Gap Report ==2025==, and ==143rd of 145== in ==2026==.

::: fig compare | Gender mainstreaming: what it promised and what it often became.
@vs vs
## The promise
- Gender in every policy and budget
- Data, analysis, accountability
- Transforming power relations
## The practice
- Checklists and workshops
- Donor-driven pilots that fade
- "Mainstreamed away"
:::

### 11. Making mainstreaming transformative in Pakistan

#### i) Institutionalise the tools
Make **gender-responsive budgeting** mandatory in federal and provincial budgets, with published gender statements; require **gender impact assessment** of major laws and projects; and fund the NCSW and provincial commissions from the public budget rather than donors.

#### ii) Combine the twin tracks
Keep **special measures** — quotas, BISP, women's police stations — alongside mainstreaming, because a gender-sensitive policy is no substitute for programmes that reach women directly.

#### iii) Link it to power and voice
Tie mainstreaming to **women's organisations**, the **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** and local women councillors, so that strategic needs — land, mobility, safety, political power — are on the agenda, and measure success by **outcomes** (girls' schooling, maternal mortality, labour participation), not by workshops held.

### 12. Conclusion
Gender mainstreaming is GAD's answer to the failure of marginal women's projects: if inequality runs through every institution, every institution must address it. It has achieved much — data, budgets, units, quotas, a common language of equality — and in Pakistan it underlies the reserved seats, the 10% job quota, BISP and the property-rights law. But without political will, resources and women's voice it becomes a ritual of checklists. Mainstreaming is a **strategy**, not a substitute for **politics**: it transforms only when it is backed by power.

## Why this answer scores
- Gives the **ECOSOC (1997)** definition and the **Beijing (1995)** origin, and links mainstreaming to **GAD** explicitly.
- Separates **analytical** tools (data, Harvard, Moser, Longwe, Kabeer) from **institutional** tools (budgeting, audits, focal points, quotas).
- **Evaluates** both sides, with Pakistani examples (GRAP, GRBI, quotas, BISP, the 2020 property law) and outcome data.
- Ends with a **way forward** and a clear judgement.
- A **flow** figure shows the policy cycle; a **compare** figure shows promise versus practice.
